1bd2-3ced-5d49-bc43-dff07a90e6dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168889 #edb/ref 1080 "5 ± 1.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168889 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168889 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d80f3c6-82c6-521a-a2a2-b01992d77cc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168890 #edb/ref 1080 "5 ± 0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168890 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168890 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7dd0969e-f100-53ab-a342-238468fad53c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169095 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "618d8cc5-09ee-52cf-bb55-128c57e46ade" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169096 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.1 ± 0.06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169096 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169096 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be3ed5e4-db77-5844-99eb-2373d53d4fe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169097 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.1 ± 0.09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169097 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169097 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c2a9c14b-c29f-53d1-8434-7046339a3295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169098 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.1 ± 0.01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169098 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169098 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ea68b01-435a-5d11-aa1b-81b29b61b328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169099 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.1 ± 0.08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169099 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169099 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "54528e56-5dcd-5b9e-93f9-76d58c3b369a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169100 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.1 ± 0.04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169100 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169100 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c6e5462-6938-5d3e-9d9a-873923c367fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171688 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171688 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.25$, and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is given by $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "416faff8-3422-5bb0-9062-571ccfb18093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171688 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7 x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 0.7$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.25}{42}} \\approx 0.3$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 0.7 \\pm 0.3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171696 #edb/ref 1069 "e-11098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171696 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they finally obtained on that exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5$, and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171696 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d33f1a3-c51f-5a97-a498-def7a8364d81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171696 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x = 21.3 + 10x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 10$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.05} = 2.92$.Computing $S_{xx}$, we get\n$∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}|= (4 - 5)^{2} + (4.5 - 5)^{2} + (5.5 - 5)^{2} + (6 - 5)^{2}; = 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 1; = 2.5$. Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 2.92 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.375}{2.5}} \\approx 2.2$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 10 \\pm 2.2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172110 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172120 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172120 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172120 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172120 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172120 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1045 "Confidence Intervals for Linear Regression Slope Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186173029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172162 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7336d11d-232f-5e59-8c98-4b8fbb77fd67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172337 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172337 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172337 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $115$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $y^(^) = 80.6 + 5x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 6$, and $MSE = 0.35$, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 4.303) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$SE[β^(^)_{1}] = \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{S_{xx}}}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172337 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ac4a55b-9276-584c-aabe-f3000169b367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172337 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172372 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Production ($x$)** | **$24$** | **$27$** | **$30$** | **$31$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Price per $kg$ ($y$)** | $2$ | $1.6$ | $1.4$ | $1.4$ |\n\nThe table above shows wheat production, in tons, and the average price of a kilogram of flour, in dollars, for a given country over the last four years. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $y^(^) = 4 - 0.1x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 28$, and $MSE = 0.007$, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$SE[β^(^)_{1}] = \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{S_{xx}}}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d548f76-b08c-5cf0-ae75-59db6390b2b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172372 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173029 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173029 #edb/ref 1061 38065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173029 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8fa03444-9380-5875-abf1-e75bbb767c7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173029 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173030 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173030 #edb/ref 1061 38066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173030 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48431442-8239-5535-a7a1-fb9068d1372d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173030 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173048 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173048 #edb/ref 1061 42154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173048 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e7ac509-670d-5307-9c8f-7ca79acc2ee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173048 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173049 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173049 #edb/ref 1061 42155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6279ed73-ac15-54d7-b41e-828f02e67018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173050 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173050 #edb/ref 1061 42157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e953bfd-106c-5024-96ce-735fef10d8c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173077 #edb/ref 1061 42308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "602e7e8d-9598-5664-953a-c538cb2fc4ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173218 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population.\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i$, and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following:\n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize,\n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$,\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1$, and\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0$.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the slope parameter $\\beta_1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173218 #edb/ref 1065 6279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173218 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "37635fea-c916-5aaa-8856-9d9f7edd8d50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173218 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173228 #edb/ref 1064 "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our slope parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$.\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$.\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nFor each\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nlet\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2$. The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i, \\sigma^2\\right)\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_1 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\: \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}} \\right)\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2$, the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_1$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_1 -\\beta_1}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}]}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173228 #edb/ref 1065 6964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173228 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "beb8e9bf-ec60-55d5-8b18-154ccf17124a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173228 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving an Expression for the Confidence Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173229 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed by fitting the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the so-called **mean squared error** of the model, given by\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$,\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by\n$(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)$.\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2$.\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173229 #edb/ref 1065 6966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173229 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7336a854-0d9f-5a8e-aaf1-b47ea3b4cff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173229 #edb/ref 1067 "Summary of the Key Result" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173249 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)nx^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our result for $S_{xy}$, we can write\n\n$$\nβ_{1}^(^) = \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, we compute the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as follows:\n\n- For the mean, we have\n$E[β_{1}^(^)]|= E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}E[y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}); = β_{0}∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i} + β_{1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{1}x_{i}; = β_{0} \\cdot 0 + β_{1} \\cdot 1; = β_{1}$.\nNote that we used the properties of $k_i$ discussed previously.\n- For the variance, we have\n$Var[β_{1}^(^)]|= Var[∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}^{2}Var[y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}^{2} \\cdot σ^{2}; = σ^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}^{2}; = \\frac{σ^{2}}{S_{xx}}$.\n\nSo, finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173249 #edb/ref 1065 7006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173249 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "719ed7ad-c96a-5299-b921-fda8c0b35e46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173249 #edb/ref 1067 "Expectation and Variance of Slope Parameter Estimates" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
